<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglo-Hanseatic War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1469">1469</a>–<a href="/years/1474">1474</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anglo-Hanseatic War was a conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, fought mainly at sea by privateers, after Hanseatic merchants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> were arrested and fined in 1468. Danzig captains such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-beneke">Paul Beneke</a> raided English shipping, and Edward IV, needing peace, agreed to the Treaty of Utrecht in 1474. The treaty restored and confirmed the Hansa's privileges in England and gave it ownership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steelyard">London Steelyard</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the 15th century English merchants competed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> for the wool and cloth trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the Baltic cities, and pressed the Crown to recognise their right to trade in the Baltic. In 1447 King Henry VI revoked all Hansa privileges, and in May 1449 English privateers captured several ships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and Danzig. An eight-year armistice was agreed in 1456 after long negotiations, but English privateers attacked Hansa shipping again from 1459, and peace talks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> failed in 1465.</p><p>Tensions rose further in 1468, when Danzig privateers sailing for the Danish Crown seized English merchant ships in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a>. King Edward IV answered by closing and seizing the Hanseatic Steelyard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Representatives of the Hanse cities meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> decided on war, banned English wool imports and ordered privateers to raid English sea routes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Danzig and, to a lesser extent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> carried the main burden of the war, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> opposed the war and was for a time excluded from the Hansa. Because the Burgundian duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> withdrew his promise to open his harbours, the Hanseatic privateers could not sail until spring 1470. Edward IV, weakened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>, had to flee to the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> in September of that year; Charles then backed his return and opened his harbours, letting the Hansa capture ships of the king's enemies, but withdrew that support once Edward was restored.</p><p>From 1472 larger Hanseatic formations, led by squadrons from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, operated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> as far as the island of Ushant, drawing out a French fleet. The war was fought mainly by commerce raiding. Among the most successful ships was the Peter von Danzig under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-beneke">Paul Beneke</a>, which from 1473 raided the English coast and attacked neutral and Burgundian ships as well.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The economic damage the war caused, seen in the decline of the wool trade during these years, was one of the main reasons it ended abruptly. Besides confirming the Hansa's privileges and giving it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steelyard">London Steelyard</a>, the Treaty of Utrecht of 1474 granted the League its trading bases in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> and Lynn. The settlement virtually halted English trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the Baltic region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anglo-Hanseatic War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Anglo-Hanseatic War</b> of 1469–1474, fought largely by privateers such as Paul Beneke of Danzig, ended favourably, but the Count’s Feud of 1534–1536 in Denmark was a costly failure for Lübeck.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Military</a></li></ul></section>
